  {"id":"sal-1","topic":"Salicylate Toxicity","domain":"Internal medicine: emergency & acute care","discipline":"medicine","stem":"What is the toxic dose threshold and mechanism of salicylate toxicity?","answer":"Toxic dose >150 mg/kg single dose or chronic high-dose use; mechanism: uncouples oxidative phosphorylation and stimulates the respiratory center","rationale":"Mitochondrial uncoupling causes metabolic acidosis; respiratory center stimulation causes hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis early.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Curated Units","page":0}],"confusable_with":""},
  {"id":"sal-2","topic":"Salicylate Toxicity","domain":"Internal medicine: emergency & acute care","discipline":"medicine","stem":"What is the classic mixed acid-base disorder in salicylate toxicity and what symptoms mark early vs late presentation?","answer":"Early: respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation), tinnitus, altered mentation, hyperthermia; Late: mixed respiratory alkalosis + metabolic acidosis; noncardiogenic pulmonary edema in severe cases","rationale":"Salicylates stimulate respiratory center (alkalosis) and uncouple oxidative phosphorylation (metabolic acidosis); both may coexist.","bloom":"analyze","source":[{"book":"Curated Units","page":0}],"confusable_with":"Pure metabolic acidosis"},
  {"id":"sal-3","topic":"Salicylate Toxicity","domain":"Internal medicine: emergency & acute care","discipline":"medicine","stem":"What is the mechanism and target of urine alkalinization in salicylate poisoning?","answer":"Sodium bicarbonate raises urine pH to 7.5-8.5; alkaline urine ionizes salicylate (charged form cannot be reabsorbed) -> enhanced renal elimination; monitor K+ closely","rationale":"Ion trapping: salicylate pKa ~3; alkaline urine keeps salicylate ionized in tubular lumen, preventing tubular reabsorption.","bloom":"analyze","source":[{"book":"Curated Units","page":0}],"confusable_with":""},
  {"id":"sal-4","topic":"Salicylate Toxicity","domain":"Internal medicine: emergency & acute care","discipline":"medicine","stem":"What are the indications for hemodialysis in salicylate toxicity?","answer":"Hemodialysis indicated if salicylate level >100 mg/dL, renal failure, severe CNS effects, pulmonary edema, or failure of urine alkalinization","rationale":"HD directly removes salicylate and corrects acid-base; indicated when elimination is impaired or toxicity is severe.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Curated Units","page":0}],"confusable_with":""},
  {"id":"sal-5","topic":"Salicylate Toxicity","domain":"Internal medicine: emergency & acute care","discipline":"medicine","stem":"What management approach addresses the noncardiogenic pulmonary edema (ARDS-like) in moderate-severe salicylate toxicity?","answer":"Restrict free water, use hypertonic saline (3%) for alkalinization instead of normal saline, judicious diuretics; PEEP 5-10 cmH2O if intubated; monitor mixed venous O2","rationale":"Direct lung toxicity plus aggressive alkalinization fluid causes edema; hypertonic saline provides bicarbonate load without volume excess.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Curated Units","page":0}],"confusable_with":""},
  {"id":"sal-6","topic":"Salicylate Toxicity","domain":"Internal medicine: emergency & acute care","discipline":"medicine","stem":"What initial gastrointestinal decontamination and alkalinization are recommended for salicylate toxicity?","answer":"Activated charcoal for GI decontamination; IV bicarbonate for serum and urine alkalinization (target serum pH 7.4-7.5, urine pH 7.5-8.5)","rationale":"Charcoal binds salicylate in gut preventing absorption; systemic alkalinization shifts salicylate into ionized non-lipid-soluble form reducing CNS penetration.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"StatPearls: StatPearls   Metabolic Acidosis  Approach and Anion Gap","page":11}],"confusable_with":""},
